# LeakSanitizer suppressions for TestLdf (set through LSAN_OPTIONS in CMakeLists.txt).
#
# The DBC classes never free what a DBCFile holds: ~DBCFile deletes only dbc_nodes, not messageHandler;
# DBCMessageHandler, DBC_MESSAGE (its sigHandler) and DBCSignalHandler have no destructors, and
# removeAllMessages() only clears the map. So every message and signal of a DBC or LDF file leaks when the
# file goes away. This is upstream behaviour and the LDF mapping (LdfToDbc) keeps it, to match.
#
# It isn't fixed here on purpose: graphs (re/graphingwindow), the search window, the signal viewer and the
# DBC editors keep raw DBC_SIGNAL / DBC_MESSAGE pointers, and DBCHandler::removeDBCFile doesn't tell them.
# The leak is what keeps those pointers valid; if a DBCFile freed its messages, a graph left open after its
# DBC is removed would decode the next frame with a freed signal (graphingwindow.cpp, processAsDouble).
#
# A pattern matches any frame of the allocation's stack, so only the places that create a file's messages
# and signals, or the strings they keep, are named. Leaks elsewhere in TestLdf (the test itself, frame file
# I/O, the rest of the LDF parser, DBC saving) are still reported and fail the test.

# messageHandler, new'd in the constructor
leak:DBCFile::DBCFile
# the DBC loader: new DBC_MESSAGE / DBC_SIGNAL (parseMessageLine, parseSignalLine), the comments, value lists
# and multiplexor lists it stores in them, and LDF files it loads through LdfToDbc
leak:DBCFile::loadFile
# the LDF mapping: new DBC_MESSAGE / DBC_SIGNAL, through makeSignal / addFrameSignals
leak:LdfToDbc::fill
# the LDF parser's names and texts: messages and signals share these strings (Qt's implicit sharing), so they
# leak with them. GCC 11's LeakSanitizer doesn't treat suppressed objects as roots (later versions do), so
# what they hold has to be named where it was allocated. These stacks run through Qt's release libraries,
# which ASan's fast unwinder can't walk, hence ASAN_OPTIONS=fast_unwind_on_malloc=0 next to LSAN_OPTIONS.
leak:tokenize
leak:itemText
# what processAsText caches in a signal (cachedValue): it goes with the signal, which is never freed
leak:DBC_SIGNAL::processAsText
